WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.400
It's great to welcome today John Thai to the
program, a former NSA whistleblower and a

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.190
lawyer who back in 2017 founded Whistleblower
Aid, which has provided support to the anonymous

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.020
Ukraine whistleblower, among many other things.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.090
John, great to have you on today.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.510
Thank you so much, David.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.330
So let's start with your path.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.330
Executive order.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.220
Twelve, three, three, three.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.720
What is it and how did that turn you into
a whistleblower?

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.270
Thanks.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.289
I was at the State Department.

00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:40.640
I worked in the Human Rights Bureau from 2011
to 2014 on Internet Freedom.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.819
And in that role, I had a top secret clearance.

00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:47.710
So I was reading intelligence from various
countries around the world.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:53.799
And in June of 2013, the Snowden disclosures
became very public news.

00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:59.829
And I was part of the diplomatic response
to those disclosures, both with Germany, Brazil,

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:05.890
other countries, the United Nations, the Human
Rights Council in Geneva.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.430
And during that period and in the nine months
following that, I got a couple of briefings

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.789
from the NSA on the scope of U.S. signals
intelligence collection.

00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:21.820
And what I learned was for that whole year
following the Snowden leaks, there hadn't

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:27.240
been a single story despite front page stories
almost every day, hadn't been a single story

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.119
on the main legal authority that the NSA is
using to collect American's data, which is,

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:35.500
as you said, executive order twelve, triple
three.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.939
I was concerned about this.

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:37.979
I was interested.

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:42.030
I obviously care very deeply about human rights
and Americans privacy.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:49.480
And so basically, this is a legal loophole
where the NSA is using an authority originally

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:58.030
intended to collect foreigners data to actually
sweep up most of what we do online, including

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.640
U.S. persons, American citizens.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.819
This concerned me.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:08.069
It wasn't being addressed in the in the policy
responses to the Snowden disclosures.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:12.080
And during that period, I became a whistleblower.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.360
I did not want to go to prison.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.160
I did not want to leave the country.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.140
And so I hired a couple of lawyers.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.670
I paid them thirteen thousand dollars.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:28.490
This would have been in twenty fourteen probably
March, April 2014 and went through the lawful

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.200
disclosure process.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:37.370
So I met with the State Department inspector
general, the NSA inspector general, House

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:43.470
and Senate Intelligence Committees and others,
and eventually went through the pre-publication

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:49.030
review process and published an op ed in The
Washington Post about this problem.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:54.980
So essentially, you believe that ordered 12
triple three was being misused in order to

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:02.250
surveil Americans when as as it was written,
you believe it was not meant to apply to Americans?

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.890
Yeah, that's right.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.200
It was originally before the Internet.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.650
There were two legal regimes, a statutory
regime for.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.960
Intelligence agency operations inside the
United States, which is under fire, that the

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.890
Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act and
then twelve triple three was meant to cover

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.260
all the operations outside of our borders.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.930
With the Internet, that distinction between
inside our borders and outside our borders

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.880
became almost irrelevant, much less relevant
and.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.060
A whole bunch of Americans data is stored
in data centers around the world and it's

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.620
treated as foreign persons, the data and collected
as such by the NSA.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.200
When we think about the government's treatment
of prominent and again, even the use of the

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.820
term whistleblower versus leaker, it's the
subject of debate and controversy.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:58.120
So I'm not trying to sort of like prime the
discussion in any particular way.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:04.590
But when we think about the treatment of folks
like Edward Snowden, Chelsea Manning, etc.,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:09.890
you have a very wide range of opinion as to
how these individuals should should be treated.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:16.670
On one side, you have the idea that strictly
speaking, if information was released in violation

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:22.230
of of how that information is classified or
if the information was obtained in a way that

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.900
violates the law or whatever, that that's
essentially all that matters that the content

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.870
really does in an individual's suit should
be punished as such.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.780
On the other side, you have those who believe
that these are merely heroic acts that should

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.020
be celebrated and acted upon to change what
is the misbehavior of the government, including,

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:43.230
as you say, the misuse of of an executive
order, executive order in the case that you

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.230
discuss.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:49.030
What is your general approach as to how individuals
should be treated by the government and by

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:50.540
the justice system?

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:57.640
Well, our motto, whistleblower aid is report
government and corporate lawbreaking without

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.260
breaking the law.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.470
So we are a legal organization.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.340
We would not never help anyone to leak classified
information.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.560
With that said, these are very difficult questions.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.880
There aren't easy answers.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:18.070
And I think reasonable people can disagree
on some of this stuff.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:25.910
You know, I we we created this organization
so that people wouldn't have to choose between

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:31.370
following their conscience and avoiding harm
to themselves.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:37.010
So so we try to help people follow the available
lawful disclosure process.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:41.430
There are often more options available than
many people realize.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:46.639
And, you know, open up more options so that
people can keep their careers, don't have

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:53.060
to go to prison and still try to try to inform
the public about about what's going wrong.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:59.020
You know, with those cases you mentioned those
you know, those people didn't feel like they

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:00.380
had other options.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:07.760
And, you know, we are here to try to help
people not have to put their career or their

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.190
freedom in jeopardy, not not to be overly
conspiratorial or dystopian or whatever.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:19.120
But one can imagine situations where one would
believe that the information they are they

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.480
are trying to disclose or what they are trying
to blow the whistle on is so damaging to the

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.090
government that going through the process
you describe might put them in jeopardy, might

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.230
put them in danger, might allow them maybe
to be silenced.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.870
So is it at least theoretically conceivable
that people could be in a position where they

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.920
want to go the route that you help people
go, but they believe that starting that process

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.960
actually puts them at risk or may prevent
the information from going public?

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.550
Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:49.550
It's a risk.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:50.850
There's no easy things here.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:55.530
And the more the more important the disclosure,
the more significant it is, the more risky

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.780
it is for the individual.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.800
And there's no way around that.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.240
We're doing everything we can to minimize
those risks.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.740
But the very first thing I tell people when
they come to me is, look, the safest thing

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.320
you can do is go home and never talk to anyone
about this again.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.480
So, you know, most people, by the time they
get to me, they've they've crossed that bridge

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:14.480
in there.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.550
They want to explore their options for going
forward.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:21.820
But, you know, it's it's risky and I would
never say otherwise.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:26.300
Can you talk a little bit for folks in our
audience who might be thinking about WikiLeaks

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:31.449
and the way that WikiLeaks has been an intermediary
with meant much information that have been

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:32.449
released?

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:36.440
Can you talk about how what you do is completely
different than what WikiLeaks does?

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.440
Absolutely.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.730
We are not WikiLeaks.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.270
We do not help people leak classified information.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.120
We would never, you know, help someone.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:53.320
Julian Assange has been accused of attempting
to help someone to to hack a government database

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:58.070
who would never help anyone trying to do that.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:06.729
You know, look, that organization has been
credibly accused of, you know, working you

00:08:06.729 --> 00:08:09.090
wittingly or unwittingly with Russian intelligence.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.460
We would never do anything like that.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.580
I swore an oath to the U.S. Constitution when
I was at the State Department.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:19.530
I'm still bound by that oath and take that
very seriously.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:24.240
So so we are very different from that approach.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:30.520
You know, with that said, I'll say I don't
agree with all of the charges against Assange

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:37.930
in the sense that, you know, if the allegations
are true and I don't know the facts, it certainly

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:42.069
would be illegal to try to help someone hack
a government database.

00:08:42.069 --> 00:08:46.869
I think publishing things receives from someone
else shouldn't is not illegal and should not

00:08:46.869 --> 00:08:48.639
be illegal.

00:08:48.639 --> 00:08:55.399
So it's a complicated legal matter when it
comes to the Ukraine whistleblower.

00:08:55.399 --> 00:08:59.259
Can you talk a little bit about do you believe
that?

00:08:59.259 --> 00:09:06.629
I mean, this is an individual who became the
target of so many prominent people in the

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:11.389
public eye in the lead up to Donald Trump's
eventual impeachment, et cetera.

00:09:11.389 --> 00:09:17.360
From your perspective, what did they go through
this process in the correct way?

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:18.370
Absolutely.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:22.180
So they immediately sought legal counsel.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:27.569
My colleague Andrew Vicci and Mark Zaid were
the lawyers for that who are part of our team

00:09:27.569 --> 00:09:30.170
here at whistleblower aid.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.470
From the very beginning, they were planning
how to do this in a lawful way that would

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:38.829
not expose the client to criminal prosecution
or even dismissal from their job inside a

00:09:38.829 --> 00:09:39.850
U.S. intelligence agency.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.800
That person is still employed.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.240
They did follow all the rules.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.110
They are entitled to legal protections.

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:53.019
I think they're an example of how the system
of how people do sometimes have more options

00:09:53.019 --> 00:09:56.920
than they than they know about the legal process.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.529
Here are very obscure.

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:02.290
Some of these statutes haven't been tested
very often.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:09.329
And so we saw that with the attempts to get
this disclosure to Congress, it was bumpy.

00:10:09.329 --> 00:10:16.240
The White House did try to block Congress
from receiving this in violation of the laws.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:21.230
But ultimately, the lawyers, Andrew and Mark,
were able to get it to Congress.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:27.740
So I certainly wouldn't say that our system
is flawless, but there are often more options

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:29.170
than people understand.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:35.149
And in this case, the client did an amazing
job and is a true hero.

00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:42.559
Were you able to or let me rephrase it a different
way before the attempts to out and create

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:45.760
backlash against the whistleblower started?

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.999
Had you already anticipated that it would
reach the degree that it did?

00:10:49.999 --> 00:10:54.640
And how did you advise the client to be prepared
for that in terms of security, in terms of

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.720
practical things?

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.610
Well, I have to be a little circumspect here,
first of all.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:06.790
I never had any direct contact with the client,
Andrew and Marc.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:09.329
My colleagues were the ones representing the
client.

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:10.329
Yeah.

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:11.329
You as they weren't duress.

00:11:11.329 --> 00:11:12.329
Yeah.

00:11:12.329 --> 00:11:13.329
Yeah.

00:11:13.329 --> 00:11:14.329
Our firm.

00:11:14.329 --> 00:11:15.329
So.

00:11:15.329 --> 00:11:18.369
So I can't confirm or deny any kind of, you
know, things about that person's identity

00:11:18.369 --> 00:11:22.059
or anything like that.

00:11:22.059 --> 00:11:27.029
Broadly speaking, as soon as it was clear
that this implicated the president and the

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:28.029
2020 election.

00:11:28.029 --> 00:11:29.029
Yes.

00:11:29.029 --> 00:11:31.980
It was obvious that this was going to be a
very high profile matter, that there were

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:34.939
gonna be security concerns from day one.

00:11:34.939 --> 00:11:41.069
We brought in top level security people on
the digital side, on the physical side, as

00:11:41.069 --> 00:11:44.430
you may know, Mark Zaid, one of the lawyers.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.699
He was getting death threats.

00:11:46.699 --> 00:11:53.959
We had armed guards on mark seed 24 hours
a day, four months.

00:11:53.959 --> 00:11:55.199
We.

00:11:55.199 --> 00:11:59.921
And now the Department of Justice is bringing
a criminal prosecution against one of the

00:11:59.921 --> 00:12:02.269
individuals who was threatening to hurt Mark.

00:12:02.269 --> 00:12:04.569
So it was a very serious, serious situation.

00:12:04.569 --> 00:12:10.300
We knew that from the beginning and from the
beginning, we were taking steps.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:16.810
That's really all I can say is it is this
once you understand the scope of what is going

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:17.810
on.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:22.009
And again, without revealing any anything
particular in general terms, do you do extra?

00:12:22.009 --> 00:12:27.069
I mean, I know that you say you always say
to a lot of the folks that come to you, the

00:12:27.069 --> 00:12:30.459
easiest thing for you is to go back home and
never speak about this again.

00:12:30.459 --> 00:12:39.100
But are there more conversations than normal
of that type given these particular circumstances?

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:44.649
I was not I never had a conversation with
this client, so I'm going to speak to that.

00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:45.720
Fair enough.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:52.739
When we look going forward, is there anything
that you believe took place?

00:12:52.739 --> 00:12:59.120
In or surrounding the Ukraine whistleblower
situation that may sort of change the way

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:03.769
these things are potentially handled in the
future, both by a firm like yours representing

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:09.050
someone like this, but also the way that Congress,
the House of Representatives and others worked

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:12.100
in receiving the information.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:14.160
Yeah.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:22.319
Clearly, threats, intimidation, starting with
the president of the United States.

00:13:22.319 --> 00:13:29.709
The president's family, senior officials,
U.S. senators, attempts to identify, intimidate

00:13:29.709 --> 00:13:35.209
this person are extremely harmful, arguably
illegal in some cases.

00:13:35.209 --> 00:13:42.209
You know, you could make the argument that
any kind of presidential effort to intimidate

00:13:42.209 --> 00:13:50.069
a whistleblower itself constitutes an offense.

00:13:50.069 --> 00:13:52.639
And.

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:55.809
So there were serious problems here.

00:13:55.809 --> 00:14:00.459
Some of those people should be held accountable
for their actions.

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:09.519
It is never right to try to identify a whistleblower
with the knowledge that, you know, some extremists

00:14:09.519 --> 00:14:14.550
would would would threaten that person, possibly
act violently against that person.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.009
There need to be stronger protections in place.

00:14:17.009 --> 00:14:22.829
Arguably, that kind of behavior should be
criminalized, which it's not today, except

00:14:22.829 --> 00:14:24.429
in certain narrow circumstances.

00:14:24.429 --> 00:14:31.420
And, you know, Congress needs to identify
the gaps that were apparent in the system

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:34.589
and and fill them with legislation.

00:14:34.589 --> 00:14:37.480
And certainly there needs to be reforms.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.920
Andrew and Mark publish The New York Times
op ed two or three weeks ago that laid out

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.369
some of their ideas for that.

00:14:43.369 --> 00:14:45.500
So clearly there's room for improvement.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:46.649
Yes.

00:14:46.649 --> 00:14:47.649
Certainly an understatement.

00:14:47.649 --> 00:14:50.350
I would say room for improvement for for sure.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:53.480
In regards to a lot of the elements of how
this all went down.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:15:00.309
John TYhe, former NSA whistleblower and attorney
who in 2017 founded Whistleblower Aid.

00:15:00.309 --> 00:15:03.730
John, so great to speak to you and I really
appreciate your time today.

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:04.790
Thanks, David.

